Since \(M\) and \(N\) divide \(L P\) into three equal parts, the length of each segment, \(L M\), \(M N\), and \(N P\), must be: \[
\frac{x_P - x_L}{3}
\]

Therefore, the position of point \(M\), which is one segment length away from \(L\), would be: \[
x_M = x_L + \frac{x_P - x_L}{3}
\]

Thus, the value at \(M\) is: \[
x_M = \frac{2x_L + x_P}{3}
\]
